cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Inscribed squares can be small: a counterexample to the big peg conjecture"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Paloviita
    given-names: Olli
version: 1.2.0
date-released: 2026-11-26
url: "https://github.com/ollipal/small-inscribed-squares"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ollipal/small-inscribed-squares"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A family of 13-gons containing a disc of radius 2m whose only inscribed square has side
  below 37 for every m >= 171, disproving the folklore big peg conjecture, and polygons showing that the outer
  radius in Matschke's annulus theorem cannot exceed 2.41423. Verified in exact arithmetic.
  Also a conjecture: every simple polygon has an inscribed square of side at least sqrt(2) r*,
  where r* is the radius of the largest disc that is not trapped inside the polygon.
keywords: [inscribed square problem, square peg problem, Toeplitz conjecture, counterexample, computational geometry]
references:
  - type: article
    title: "The trapped ball: a conjecture on the size of inscribed squares"
    authors:
      - family-names: Paloviita
        given-names: Olli
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/ollipal/small-inscribed-squares/blob/main/paper/conjecture.pdf"
